Recognition: the problem asks about a [[o:contiguous sequence]] (substring / subarray) that satisfies some property, and that property is [[g:monotonic]] — expanding the window can only make it "worse," while shrinking it can only make it "better." The pointers therefore never move backward → O(n).

**Variable-size (dynamic)** — the standard template:

<pre><code><span class="k">int</span> left = <span class="n">0</span>, best = <span class="n">0</span>;
<span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> right = <span class="n">0</span>; right &lt; n; right++) {
    add(a[right]);                      <span class="c">// expand on the right</span>
    <span class="k">while</span> (!valid()) {                 <span class="c">// invalid =&gt; shrink from the left</span>
        remove(a[left]);
        left++;
    }
    best = Math.<span class="f">max</span>(best, right - left + <span class="n">1</span>);
}</code></pre>

**Fixed-size (constant k)** — no `while` is needed; just slide:

<pre><code><span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> right = <span class="n">0</span>; right &lt; n; right++) {
    add(a[right]);
    <span class="k">if</span> (right &gt;= k) remove(a[right - k]);   <span class="c">// remove the element that fell out</span>
    <span class="k">if</span> (right &gt;= k - <span class="n">1</span>) record(left = right - k + <span class="n">1</span>);
}</code></pre>

Each element enters the window exactly once and leaves exactly once → [[g:O(n)]], even with a `while` nested inside the `for`. Interviewers often probe this point: *"Why is a while loop nested inside a for loop still O(n)?"*

:::deep
- The [[r:necessary]] condition for sliding window to work is that the property be monotonic with respect to window length. An array with [[r:negative numbers]] breaks that condition for "subarray sum ≥ k" → use prefix sums plus a deque or binary search, not sliding window.
- Shrinking with `while` versus `if`: use `while` for a "*longest* valid window" problem; use `if` for a fixed-size window.
- Window state is typically a frequency-counting `HashMap`, a running sum, or a monotonic `Deque` for window maximum/minimum problems.
- The "exactly k distinct characters" variant = `atMost(k) - atMost(k-1)` — a useful and frequently tested trick.
- Classic progression by difficulty: LC 3 (no repeated characters) → LC 76 (minimum window substring) → LC 30 (19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q3) → LC 239 (sliding window maximum, which requires a deque).
:::

**Problem:** given `s` and an array `words` (every word has [[o:the same length]] `w`, and duplicates are allowed), find every starting index of a substring formed by concatenating [[g:all]] the words in `words`, using each word exactly once with no intervening characters.

`s = "barfoothefoobarman"`, `words = ["foo","bar"]` → `[0, 9]`

**Key insight:** because every word has the same length `w`, the string can be viewed as a sequence of [[o:tokens]] — but there are `w` different ways to align those tokens (starting at offsets 0, 1, ... w−1). Run an [[g:independent sliding window for each offset]], advancing `w` characters at every step.

**Brute force** — try every position `i`, slice out `k` words, and compare frequency counters: O(n × k × w). State this approach first.

**Optimized — a word-level sliding window:**

<pre><code><span class="k">public</span> List&lt;Integer&gt; <span class="f">findSubstring</span>(String s, String[] words) {
    <span class="k">int</span> k = words.length, w = words[<span class="n">0</span>].<span class="f">length</span>();
    List&lt;Integer&gt; res = <span class="k">new</span> ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    <span class="k">if</span> (s.<span class="f">length</span>() &lt; (<span class="k">long</span>) k * w) <span class="k">return</span> res;
    Map&lt;String,Integer&gt; need = <span class="k">new</span> HashMap&lt;&gt;();
    <span class="k">for</span> (String x : words) need.<span class="f">merge</span>(x, <span class="n">1</span>, Integer::sum);
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> off = <span class="n">0</span>; off &lt; w; off++) {          <span class="c">// w token alignments</span>
        Map&lt;String,Integer&gt; win = <span class="k">new</span> HashMap&lt;&gt;();
        <span class="k">int</span> left = off, formed = <span class="n">0</span>;
        <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> r = off; r + w &lt;= s.<span class="f">length</span>(); r += w) {
            String word = s.<span class="f">substring</span>(r, r + w);
            <span class="k">if</span> (!need.<span class="f">containsKey</span>(word)) {          <span class="c">// unknown word =&gt; reset the entire window</span>
                win.<span class="f">clear</span>(); formed = <span class="n">0</span>; left = r + w;
                <span class="k">continue</span>;
            }
            win.<span class="f">merge</span>(word, <span class="n">1</span>, Integer::sum); formed++;
            <span class="k">while</span> (win.<span class="f">get</span>(word) &gt; need.<span class="f">get</span>(word)) {  <span class="c">// excess =&gt; shrink from the left</span>
                String out = s.<span class="f">substring</span>(left, left + w);
                win.<span class="f">merge</span>(out, -<span class="n">1</span>, Integer::sum);
                formed--; left += w;
            }
            <span class="k">if</span> (formed == k) {                       <span class="c">// complete =&gt; record it</span>
                res.<span class="f">add</span>(left);
                String out = s.<span class="f">substring</span>(left, left + w);
                win.<span class="f">merge</span>(out, -<span class="n">1</span>, Integer::sum);
                formed--; left += w;                 <span class="c">// slide by exactly 1 word</span>
            }
        }
    }
    <span class="k">return</span> res;
}</code></pre>

**Complexity:** the outer loop runs `w` times, the inner loop takes `n/w` steps, and each substring extraction costs O(w) → [[g:O(n × w)]] time and [[g:O(k × w)]] space. When `w` is small (the LC constraint is ≤ 30), the implementation is effectively linear in `n`.

:::warn Three easy mistakes
`words` [[r:may contain duplicates]] (`["word","good","best","word"]`) → count frequencies; using a `Set` is incorrect. On an unknown word, [[r:reset the entire]] window instead of shrinking it gradually. And once the window contains `k` words, [[r:still slide its left edge by exactly one word]] and continue — do not `break`, because the next position may also be valid (`"aaaaaa"`, `["aa","aa"]` → `[0,1,2]`).
:::

:::deep
Why the `off` loop must run from 0 through w−1: the window advances only in multiples of `w`, so a window starting at 0 can never reach positions 1, 2, and so on. Each offset is an independent "lane," and the w lanes cover every position.

`formed` (the number of valid words currently in the window) avoids comparing the two maps at every step — this is what reduces O(n·k) to O(n).

**Test cases to volunteer proactively:**
- `s="barfoothefoobarman"`, `["foo","bar"]` → `[0,9]`
- `s="wordgoodgoodgoodbestword"`, `["word","good","best","word"]` → `[]` (duplicate word)
- `s="aaaaaa"`, `["aa","aa"]` → `[0,1,2]` (overlapping results)
- `s="a"`, `["a"]` → `[0]` (boundary case)
- `s` shorter than `k*w` → `[]` (early exit)

**Further optimization if asked:** replace each `String` key with a [[o:numeric ID]] (map every word to an `int` through a prebuilt `HashMap`) → comparison and hashing become much cheaper than `String.substring` + `hashCode`. Alternatively, use a rolling hash to avoid allocating substrings. Mentioning both directions earns credit.

[[b:General recognition pattern]]: "all words, each exactly once, contiguous" + "every word has the same length" = a sliding window over tokens. This belongs to the same family as LC 76 (minimum window substring) and LC 438 (find all anagrams); only the unit of movement changes from a *character* to a *word*.
:::

**Canonical problem:** count the subarrays whose sum is exactly `k`, where the array [[r:may contain negative numbers]].

Sliding window [[r:fails]] here: with negative numbers, expanding the window no longer increases the sum monotonically → there is no reliable signal for when to shrink it.

**Idea:** let `P[i]` be the prefix sum through `i`. The subarray `(j, i]` has sum `k` ⟺ `P[i] − P[j] = k` ⟺ `P[j] = P[i] − k`. We therefore only need to count how many times we have already seen the value `P[i] − k`.

<pre><code><span class="k">int</span> <span class="f">subarraySum</span>(<span class="k">int</span>[] a, <span class="k">int</span> k) {
    Map&lt;Integer,Integer&gt; seen = <span class="k">new</span> HashMap&lt;&gt;();
    seen.<span class="f">put</span>(<span class="n">0</span>, <span class="n">1</span>);              <span class="c">// empty prefix: important!</span>
    <span class="k">int</span> sum = <span class="n">0</span>, count = <span class="n">0</span>;
    <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> x : a) {
        sum += x;
        count += seen.<span class="f">getOrDefault</span>(sum - k, <span class="n">0</span>);
        seen.<span class="f">merge</span>(sum, <span class="n">1</span>, Integer::sum);
    }
    <span class="k">return</span> count;
}</code></pre>

O(n) time and O(n) space. The `seen.put(0, 1)` line handles the case where the prefix from the beginning already sums to `k` — [[r:forget it and the first test case fails immediately]].

:::deep
The same template produces several other solutions by changing what you store or count:

- **Sum divisible by k** → store `sum % k` (remember to normalize negative values: `((sum % k) + k) % k`)
- **Longest subarray with sum = k** → map each prefix sum to the [[o:first index]] where it appears, and insert only when it is absent
- **Equal numbers of 0s and 1s** → convert every 0 to −1, reducing the problem to sum = 0
- **Parity and XOR problems** → use prefix XOR with the analogous equation `P[i] ^ P[j] = k`

- In 2D, use two-dimensional prefix sums + inclusion–exclusion for O(1) rectangle-sum queries.
- If dynamic updates are required, replace the prefix array with a [[o:Fenwick tree / BIT]] so both queries and updates are O(log n).
- [[r:Integer overflow]]: summing 10⁵ elements as large as 10⁹ exceeds `int` → use `long`. Interviewers frequently probe this exact issue.
- State the boundary clearly: sliding window for [[g:non-negative arrays]], prefix sum + hash map for [[g:arrays that may contain negatives]]. Articulating this distinction is a strong signal.
:::
